\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A rectangular cuboid has three mutually perpendicular edges of positive lengths $a,b,c$. A perfect cuboid requires these edge lengths, its three face diagonal lengths $d_{ab},d_{ac},d_{bc}$, and its space diagonal length $D$ all to be positive integers.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Do there exist $a,b,c,d_{ab},d_{ac},d_{bc},D\in\mathbb N$ satisfying simultaneously
\[
 a^2+b^2=d_{ab}^2,\qquad a^2+c^2=d_{ac}^2,\qquad b^2+c^2=d_{bc}^2,
 \qquad a^2+b^2+c^2=D^2?
\]
All three edges must be nonzero; degenerate planar boxes are excluded. No primitive or ordering condition is imposed. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.06784v1}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does any genuine rectangular box have integer lengths for all three edges, all three face diagonals and its space diagonal?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 1456 (ALG-011). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Aubrey D. N. J. de Grey, Philip Gibbs and Louie Helm, Novel Required Properties of, and Efficient Algorithms to Seek, Perfect Cuboids, arXiv:2401.06784v1 (2024), Section 1, definition of a perfect cuboid. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/2401.06784v1}.
\end{description}
